{"content":[{"signature":"","thinking":"\nLet me analyze this task carefully.\n\nThe task SE-PE-01 requires me to write a `solve(case)` function that takes a payload with:\n- `nodes`: a list of nodes, each with `id`, `parent`, `symbol`, `depth`, `removable`, `utility`\n- `required_symbols`: a list of symbols that must be retained\n\nThe output must have:\n- `removed_node_ids`: list of node IDs that were removed\n- `retained_node_ids`: list of node IDs that were retained\n\nLet me analyze the fixtures to understand the pruning logic:\n\nEach node has:\n- `id`: unique identifier\n- `parent`: parent node ID (null for root)\n- `symbol`: the symbol type\n- `depth`: depth in tree\n- `removable`: whether this node can be removed\n- `utility`: a float value\n\n`required_symbols`: symbols that must be present in the retained tree.\n\nThe task is about pruning a tree. The key constraints seem to be:\n1. Nodes with `removable: false` cannot be removed\n2. Nodes whose symbol is in `required_symbols` must be retained (at least one of each)\n3. When a node is removed, its entire subtree is removed\n\nWait, let me think more carefully. Looking at the fixtures:\n\nCase 000:\n- required_symbols: [\"root\", \"s1\"]\n- Nodes with symbol \"s1\": n1 (removable: false), n4 (removable: true, parent: n2)\n- Nodes with symbol \"root\": root (removable: false)\n- Nodes with symbol \"s2\": n2 (removable: true), n5 (removable: false, parent: n3)\n- Nodes with symbol \"s0\": n3 (removable: false), n6 (removable: true, parent: n1)\n\nSince `required_symbols` are [\"root\", \"s1\"], we need to keep at least one node of each required symbol. \"root\" is always there. \"s1\" is n1 (non-removable) and n4 (removable, child of n2).\n\nSince n1 is not removable, it must stay. n4 is removable but its parent n2 is removable too.\n\nThe question is: what's the optimization criterion? We want to minimize/maximize something while satisfying constraints.\n\nGiven the `utility` field, this is likely an optimization problem: maximize total utility of retained nodes, or minimize the number of retained nodes, while ensuring:\n1. All non-removable nodes are retained\n2. At least one node of each required_symbol is retained\n3. If a node is retained, its ancestors must be retained (tree connectivity)\n4. If a node is removed, all its descendants can be removed\n\nWait, actually let me reconsider. The task says \"pruning\" - this is likely about which subtrees to prune to maximize utility or something.\n\nLet me think about this differently. The problem might be:\n- We want to prune the tree optimally\n- A node can only be removed if `removable: true`\n- If we remove a node, all its descendants are also removed\n- We need to ensure all `required_symbols` are still present in the retained tree\n- The goal is to maximize total utility of retained nodes (or perhaps minimize retained nodes while keeping required symbols)\n\nActually, looking at the utility values and the problem structure, I think the goal might be to maximize the total utility by removing subtrees that have low utility, while:\n1. Keeping all non-removable nodes\n2. Ensuring all required_symbols have at least one representative in the retained set\n\nLet me think about what makes sense as an optimization:\n\nFor case 000:\n- Non-removable nodes: root, n1, n3, n5 (must be retained)\n- Removable nodes: n2, n4, n6\n- required_symbols: [\"root\", \"s1\"]\n  - \"root\" covered by root (non-removable) ✓\n  - \"s1\" covered by n1 (non-removable, depth 1) ✓\n  \nSince all required symbols are already covered by non-removable nodes, we can freely remove any removable nodes.\n\nIf we want to maximize utility: we'd keep nodes with high utility.\n- n2 utility: 0.546, and removing n2 also removes n4 (utility: 0.665)\n- n4 utility: 0.665 (child of n2)\n- n6 utility: 0.195 (child of n1)\n\nIf we maximize total utility:\n- Keep n2 + n4: 0.546 + 0.665 = 1.211\n- Remove n6: 0.195\n\nSo total utility = root(1.0) + n1(0.875) + n2(0.546) + n3(0.436) + n4(0.665) + n5(0.701) + [keep n6: 0.195 or not]\n\nActually since we're maximizing, we'd keep all positive utility nodes. All utilities are positive, so we'd keep everything!\n\nHmm, that doesn't make sense as an optimization. Let me reconsider.\n\nMaybe the optimization is to remove as many nodes as possible (maximize removed) while keeping required symbols covered. Or maybe it's a minimum cost pruning.\n\nOr maybe the logic is simpler: remove all removable nodes whose subtree doesn't contain any required symbol that isn't already covered elsewhere.\n\nLet me think about it differently. The problem might be:\n- Required symbols must each appear at least once in the retained tree\n- Non-removable nodes stay\n- For removable nodes, remove them if removing their subtree doesn't violate the required_symbols constraint\n\nActually, I think the key insight might be: we want to prune subtrees that are \"redundant\" - i.e., the required symbol coverage they provide is already covered elsewhere.\n\nFor case 000:\n- required_symbols: [\"root\", \"s1\"]\n- \"root\" is covered by the root node (non-removable)\n- \"s1\" is covered by n1 (non-removable, depth 1) AND by n4 (removable, depth 2, child of n2)\n\nn2's subtree contains n4 (symbol s1). But s1 is already covered by n1. So we can prune n2 (and n4 with it).\nn6 is symbol s0, which is not in required_symbols, so we can prune n6.\n\nSo for case 000: removed = {n2, n4, n6}, retained = {root, n1, n3, n5}\n\nFor case 001 (same structure, different utilities):\n- Same logic would apply: n2 and n4 are removable, n4 is s1 but s1 is already covered by n1\n- n6 is s0, not required\n- So removed = {n2, n4, n6}, retained = {root, n1, n3, n5}\n\nFor case 002 (same structure):\n- Same logic: removed = {n2, n4, n6}, retained = {root, n1, n3, n5}\n\nWait, but all three cases have the same tree structure (just different utilities)! So if the logic doesn't depend on utility, all cases would give the same result.\n\nLet me reconsider. Maybe utility matters for the decision.\n\nActually, maybe the problem is: maximize utility of the retained subtree, subject to:\n1. All non-removable nodes are retained\n2. All required symbols are covered\n3. If a node is retained, its parent is retained (connectivity)\n4. Only removable nodes can be removed\n\nFor case 000:\nNon-removable: root(1.0), n1(0.875), n3(0.436), n5(0.701)\nRemovable: n2(0.546), n4(0.665, child of n2), n6(0.195, child of n1)\n\nDecisions:\n- n2: removable. If we keep n2, we can optionally keep n4.\n  - Keep n2, keep n4: gain = 0.546 + 0.665 = 1.211\n  - Keep n2, remove n4: gain = 0.546 (n4 is removable and doesn't add required symbols)\n  - Wait, but n4's symbol is s1, which is in required_symbols. But s1 is already covered by n1.\n  - Remove n2 (and n4 with it): gain = 0\n\nSince all utilities are positive, we'd always want to keep nodes. So this doesn't make sense as a max utility problem.\n\nUnless the objective is to MINIMIZE the number of retained nodes (minimize the tree size) while covering all required symbols.\n\nFor case 000:\n- Must keep: root, n1, n3, n5 (non-removable)\n- Already covers: root (symbol root), n1 (symbol s1), n3 (symbol s0), n5 (symbol s2)\n- required_symbols = [\"root\", \"s1\"] - both already covered by non-removable nodes\n- So we can remove all removable nodes: n2, n4, n6\n- removed = [n2, n4, n6], retained = [root, n1, n3, n5]\n\nThis seems consistent. But then utility doesn't matter at all.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the problem is: maximize utility, and since we can only remove nodes that are removable, and removing a node removes its subtree, we find the optimal pruning.\n\nBut if all utilities are positive, we'd keep everything... unless there are cases where utilities can be negative (not in these fixtures).\n\nHmm, or maybe the objective is to minimize total utility of the pruned subtree? No, that doesn't make sense.\n\nLet me re-read the problem. It says \"SE-PE-01\" which could be \"Symbol Elimination - Pruning Exploration\" or something similar. \n\nActually wait - maybe the goal is specifically: remove all removable nodes whose SUBTREE does not contain any required symbol that is NOT already covered by the non-removable portion of the tree, or by other higher-priority branches.\n\nActually, I think the simplest interpretation that matches all cases is:\n\n**Algorithm**: Remove a removable node (and its subtree) if and only if removing it doesn't leave any required_symbol uncovered. Process from leaves to root (bottom-up) to be greedy.\n\nFor case 000:\n- required_symbols: [\"root\", \"s1\"]\n- Bottom-up traversal:\n  - n6 (symbol s0, removable): s0 not in required_symbols → remove\n  - n4 (symbol s1, removable): s1 in required_symbols. Is s1 covered elsewhere? Yes, by n1 (non-removable) → remove\n  - n5 (symbol s2, non-removable): keep\n  - n2 (symbol s2, removable): s2 not in required_symbols → remove (but n4 already removed)\n  - n1 (symbol s1, non-removable): keep\n  - n3 (symbol s0, non-removable): keep\n  - root: keep\n\nremoved = [n2, n4, n6], retained = [root, n1, n3, n5]\n\nFor cases 001 and 002, same tree structure → same result.\n\nBut wait, what if utility matters? Maybe when a required symbol would be \"lost\" by removing a node, we use utility to decide between alternatives?\n\nActually, looking at these test cases, all three have the same tree topology and required_symbols, just different utility values. The structure-based algorithm would give the same answer for all three. But if utility matters, the answers might differ.\n\nLet me think about a case where utility WOULD matter: if removing a node would leave a required symbol uncovered (only covered by that subtree), then we can't remove it. But what if we need to choose between two subtrees to remove?\n\nHmm, I think the most reasonable interpretation is:\n\n**Prune (remove) a node and its subtree if:**\n1. The node is removable\n2. Removing its subtree doesn't leave any required_symbol without coverage (i.e., for every required_symbol covered by this subtree, there's at least one other non-removed node with that symbol)\n\n**Greedily**: process from most promising removals first (perhaps by utility - remove low utility subtrees first? Or by depth - leaves first?).\n\nBut since in all three test cases the answer would be the same regardless of utility (n2, n4, n6 can all be removed without affecting required symbol coverage), utility might just be a red herring for these test cases, or it's used as a tiebreaker in more complex cases.\n\nLet me think about this more carefully. I'll go with the greedy approach:\n\n1. Build the tree structure\n2. For each removable node (in some order), check if removing its subtree would violate required_symbol coverage\n3. If not, remove it\n4. Otherwise, keep it\n\nThe order might matter. Let me think... if we process bottom-up (leaves first), we might be able to remove more nodes.\n\nActually, I think the cleanest interpretation is:\n\n**Find the minimum set of nodes to retain** such that:\n1. All non-removable nodes are retained\n2. All required_symbols have at least one retained node\n3. The retained set forms a connected subtree (all ancestors of retained nodes are retained)\n\nThis is equivalent to: we CAN remove a node only if it's removable, and we MUST keep a node if:\n- It's non-removable, OR  \n- It's the only node with a required_symbol that would otherwise be uncovered, OR\n- An ancestor of a node that must be kept (but since non-removable nodes are always in the tree, this is about paths)\n\nWait but actually if a non-removable node is already in the tree and its ancestors are non-removable (or the chain from root to this node has all non-removable), then we don't need to worry about connectivity.\n\nActually, can a removable node be an ancestor of a non-removable node? Yes! For example, if n2 were non-removable but its parent n2 itself were removable... wait, n2 is the parent here.\n\nHmm, in these fixtures, the non-removable nodes have removable parents in some cases? Let me check:\n- n5 (non-removable, parent: n3 which is non-removable) ✓\n- n1 (non-removable, parent: root which is non-removable) ✓\n- n3 (non-removable, parent: root) ✓\n- n6 (removable, parent: n1 which is non-removable) - n6 is removable, its parent n1 is non-removable\n\nWhat if a non-removable node had a removable parent? Then we'd be forced to keep the removable parent too (connectivity constraint).\n\nFor now, in these test cases, I believe the algorithm is:\n\n1. Mark all non-removable nodes as \"must keep\"\n2. For each required_symbol, if no non-removable node has that symbol, then at least one removable node with that symbol must be kept (pick highest utility one, keeping its ancestors)\n3. All other removable nodes can be removed\n\nThen:\n- removed_node_ids: all removable nodes not in the \"must keep\" set\n- retained_node_ids: all non-removable nodes + \"must keep\" removable nodes\n\nBut we also need to ensure: if we must keep a removable node, we must keep its ancestors (even if they're removable). This could cascade.\n\nLet me code this up:\n\n```python\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case[\"nodes\"]\n    required_symbols = set(case[\"required_symbols\"])\n    \n    # Build lookup structures\n    node_by_id = {n[\"id\"]: n for n in nodes}\n    children = {n[\"id\"]: [] for n in nodes}\n    for n in nodes:\n        if n[\"parent\"] is not None:\n            children[n[\"parent\"]].append(n[\"id\"])\n    \n    # Find root\n    root_id = next(n[\"id\"] for n in nodes if n[\"parent\"] is None)\n    \n    # Determine which nodes must be kept\n    # A non-removable node must always be kept\n    must_keep = set(n[\"id\"] for n in nodes if not n[\"removable\"])\n    \n    # For each required symbol, check if it's covered by non-removable nodes\n    # If not, find the best removable node to cover it\n    symbols_covered_by_non_removable = set(\n        n[\"symbol\"] for n in nodes if not n[\"removable\"]\n    )\n    \n    uncovered_required = required_symbols - symbols_covered_by_non_removable\n    \n    for symbol in uncovered_required:\n        # Find all nodes with this symbol that are removable\n        candidates = [n for n in nodes if n[\"symbol\"] == symbol and n[\"removable\"]]\n        if candidates:\n            # Pick the one with highest utility\n            best = max(candidates, key=lambda n: n[\"utility\"])\n            # Keep this node and all its ancestors\n            node_id = best[\"id\"]\n            while node_id is not None:\n                must_keep.add(node_id)\n                node_id = node_by_id[node_id][\"parent\"]\n    \n    # Now determine retained and removed\n    retained = []\n    removed = []\n    \n    # A node is retained if it's in must_keep, or if it's... \n    # Wait, what about non-required removable nodes? They can be removed.\n    # But what if a removable node is an ancestor of a must_keep node?\n    # We need to ensure ancestors of must_keep nodes are also kept.\n    \n    # Actually, the must_keep propagation above (while node_id is not None) handles this.\n    # But for non-removable nodes that are always kept, their ancestors need to be kept too.\n    # But non-removable nodes' ancestors should all be non-removable (by definition of a well-formed tree?)\n    # Not necessarily...\n    \n    # Let's ensure all ancestors of must_keep nodes are also kept\n    # (already handled for required symbols, but let's do it for non-removable nodes too)\n    \n    # Actually, for non-removable nodes, we already mark them as must_keep.\n    # But if a non-removable node has a removable ancestor, that ancestor also must be kept.\n    \n    # Let's propagate: for every node in must_keep, mark all its ancestors as must_keep too\n    initial_must_keep = set(must_keep)\n    for node_id in initial_must_keep:\n        current = node_by_id[node_id][\"parent\"]\n        while current is not None:\n            must_keep.add(current)\n            current = node_by_id[current][\"parent\"]\n    \n    for n in nodes:\n        if n[\"id\"] in must_keep:\n            retained.append(n[\"id\"])\n        else:\n            removed.append(n[\"id\"])\n    \n    return {\n        \"removed_node_ids\": removed,\n        \"retained_node_ids\": retained\n    }\n```\n\nLet me trace through case 000:\n- Non-removable: root, n1, n3, n5 → must_keep = {root, n1, n3, n5}\n- Symbols covered by non-removable: {root, s1, s0, s2}\n- required_symbols = {\"root\", \"s1\"}\n- uncovered_required = {} (empty, both covered)\n- No additional nodes needed\n- Propagate ancestors: root has no parent. n1's parent is root (already in must_keep). n3's parent is root. n5's parent is n3.\n- must_keep = {root, n1, n3, n5}\n- Retained: [root, n1, n3, n5]\n- Removed: [n2, n4, n6]\n\nThat seems correct!\n\nFor cases 001 and 002 (same structure): same result.\n\nBut wait - I'm not using utility at all in the case where there IS a required symbol only covered by removable nodes. In that case, I pick max utility. But for these test cases it doesn't matter since all required symbols are covered by non-removable nodes.\n\nAlso, I need to think about: what if we must keep a removable node to cover a required symbol, but that forces us to keep its removable ancestors? And one of those ancestors has children that are removable and don't contribute to required symbols - those children can still be removed.\n\nI think the algorithm is correct. Let me also consider: should we try to remove all possible removable nodes that aren't needed? Yes, I think the goal is minimal retention.\n\nLet me also think about ordering of the output lists. The problem doesn't specify an order, so I'll just output them in the order they appear in the input nodes list.\n\nLet me refine the code:\n\n```python\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case[\"nodes\"]\n    required_symbols = set(case[\"required_symbols\"])\n    \n    # Build lookup\n    node_by_id = {n[\"id\"]: n for n in nodes}\n    \n    # Step 1: Non-removable nodes must be kept\n    must_keep = set(n[\"id\"] for n in nodes if not n[\"removable\"])\n    \n    # Step 2: Ensure required symbols are covered\n    symbols_covered_by_non_removable = set(\n        n[\"symbol\"] for n in nodes if not n[\"removable\"]\n    )\n    \n    uncovered_required = required_symbols - symbols_covered_by_non_removable\n    \n    for symbol in uncovered_required:\n        candidates = [n for n in nodes if n[\"symbol\"] == symbol and n[\"removable\"]]\n        if candidates:\n            best = max(candidates, key=lambda n: n[\"utility\"])\n            # Keep this node and its ancestors\n            nid = best[\"id\"]\n            while nid is not None:\n                must_keep.add(nid)\n                nid = node_by_id[nid][\"parent\"]\n    \n    # Step 3: Propagate must_keep to ancestors (for non-removable nodes with removable ancestors)\n    # (This handles any removable ancestor of a must_keep node)\n    queue = list(must_keep)\n    for nid in queue:\n        parent = node_by_id[nid][\"parent\"]\n        if parent is not None and parent not in must_keep:\n            must_keep.add(parent)\n            queue.append(parent)\n    \n    # Build result\n    retained = [n[\"id\"] for n in nodes if n[\"id\"] in must_keep]\n    removed = [n[\"id\"] for n in nodes if n[\"id\"] not in must_keep]\n    \n    return {\n        \"removed_node_ids\": removed,\n        \"retained_node_ids\": retained\n    }\n```\n\nThis looks solid. Let me verify once more with case 000:\n\nnodes: root, n1, n2, n3, n4, n5, n6\nrequired_symbols: {root, s1}\n\nmust_keep after step 1: {root, n1, n3, n5}\n\nsymbols_covered_by_non_removable: {root (root), s1 (n1), s0 (n3), s2 (n5)}\n\nuncovered_required: {root, s1} - {root, s1, s0, s2} = {} \n\nStep 3 propagation: all must_keep nodes' ancestors are already in must_keep or are null.\n\nretained: [root, n1, n3, n5]\nremoved: [n2, n4, n6]\n\nThis seems right!\n\nLet me write the final implementation cleanly.\n","type":"thinking"},{"text":"{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case[\\\"nodes\\\"]\\n    required_symbols = set(case[\\\"required_symbols\\\"])\\n\\n    # Build id -\u003e node lookup\\n    node_by_id = {n[\\\"id\\\"]: n for n in nodes}\\n\\n    # Step 1: non-removable nodes must always be kept\\n    must_keep = set(n[\\\"id\\\"] for n in nodes if not n[\\\"removable\\\"])\\n\\n    # Step 2: ensure every required symbol is covered by at least one retained node\\n    symbols_covered_by_non_removable = set(\\n        n[\\\"symbol\\\"] for n in nodes if not n[\\\"removable\\\"]\\n    )\\n    uncovered = required_symbols - symbols_covered_by_non_removable\\n\\n    for symbol in uncovered:\\n        # Among removable nodes with this symbol, pick highest utility\\n        candidates = [n for n in nodes if n[\\\"symbol\\\"] == symbol and n[\\\"removable\\\"]]\\n        if candidates:\\n            best = max(candidates, key=lambda n: n[\\\"utility\\\"])\\n            # Keep this node and walk up to root to maintain tree connectivity\\n            nid = best[\\\"id\\\"]\\n            while nid is not None:\\n                must_keep.add(nid)\\n                nid = node_by_id[nid][\\\"parent\\\"]\\n\\n    # Step 3: propagate must_keep upward in case any non-removable node\\n    # has a removable ancestor (ensure connectivity)\\n    queue = list(must_keep)\\n    idx = 0\\n    while idx \u003c len(queue):\\n        nid = queue[idx]\\n        idx += 1\\n        parent = node_by_id[nid][\\\"parent\\\"]\\n        if parent is not None and parent not in must_keep:\\n            must_keep.add(parent)\\n            queue.append(parent)\\n\\n    # Preserve original node order for deterministic output\\n    retained = [n[\\\"id\\\"] for n in nodes if n[\\\"id\\\"] in must_keep]\\n    removed = [n[\\\"id\\\"] for n in nodes if n[\\\"id\\\"] not in must_keep]\\n\\n    return {\\n        \\\"removed_node_ids\\\": removed,\\n        \\\"retained_node_ids\\\": retained\\n    }\\n\"}","type":"text"}],"id":"msg_01AGLWuIWJ7BP1nesTfUWuB0","model":"claude-opus-4-7","role":"assistant","stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"type":"message","usage":{"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2235},"cache_creation_input_tokens":2235,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"inference_geo":"not_available","input_tokens":42,"output_tokens":6168,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":5620},"service_tier":"standard"}}